title: 'k8s_apply: 409 detection is still a substring regex although Kubernetes::REST
  1.109 throws APIError'
updated: 2026-10-19T20:31:23Z
---

`_is_conflict_error` (lib/MCP/K8s.pm:550-580) matches `/409|AlreadyExists/i` against the stringified error. k1 kept the regex because Kubernetes::REST 1.107/1.108 croaked plain strings ('Revisit when the client grows typed errors'). The cpanfile now requires Kubernetes::REST 1.109, whose `_check_response` throws `Kubernetes::REST::APIError` with `code`, `reason` and `is_conflict` (kubernetes-rest lib/Kubernetes/REST.pm:1536-1554, lib/Kubernetes/REST/APIError.pm). The premise of the k1 resolution is gone, and the POD (lib/MCP/K8s.pm:561-574) and CLAUDE.md ('typisierte Fehler gibt es nur in den v0-Kompatibilitaetsmodulen') describe a state that no longer exists.

The wide match produces false positives on any create error whose body merely contains '409':

```
perl -Ilib -MMCP::K8s -e 'my $k=MCP::K8s->new(namespaces=>["default"]); print $k->_is_conflict_error(q{Kubernetes API error (create X): 422 {"message":"Service \"svc-4090\" is invalid: spec.ports: Required value","code":422}})'   # 1
```

Same for a 403 naming `system:serviceaccount:ns:sa-1409`. Effect in `k8s_apply` (lib/MCP/K8s.pm:1534-1553): the real validation/authorization error is discarded, a strategic patch is sent, and the LLM is told 'Failed to update X/name: 404 ...' for an object that never existed.

Done when: `k8s_apply` branches on `ref $@ && $@->isa('Kubernetes::REST::APIError') && $@->is_conflict` (keeping a string fallback only for non-object errors if wanted), POD/CLAUDE.md are updated, and t/05 has a 422-with-'409'-in-body case that must not fall through to patch.

